invariant: INV-16
file: p256_wide_word.h
catches: test/lint-p256-wide.sh
reason: the header picks the overflow builtins for gcc on x86-64, where
  it must pick the two intrinsics. gcc for another machine still reads
  the 128-bit sums, so only the x86-64 reading shows it. Every value
  stays right, and at -O2 gcc's if-conversion passes remove the jumps
  it makes of the builtins, so no test binary and no count objects.
  tools/p256-wide-carry.py reads the header as gcc reads it for x86-64
  and refuses a builtin there.

--- old
#elif defined(__x86_64__)
#define P256_WIDE_CARRY P256_WIDE_CARRY_INTRINSIC
--- new
#elif defined(__x86_64__)
#define P256_WIDE_CARRY P256_WIDE_CARRY_BUILTIN
